    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d659">

                <pb facs="0818" n="708" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg708"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3340: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-29T15:00:00+08:00">December 29, 1931—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 29—6:17 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1146. The Military Attaché at Chinchow telegraphs that the railway company has
                    been ordered by the Chinese military authorities to assemble 6 locomotives and
                    150 cars at Chinchow, and it is his opinion that the Chinese troops are on the
                    point of withdrawing within the Great Wall.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d659-f1"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d659-f1" n="9" xml:id="d659-f1-content">
                <p>For decision to withdraw, see telegram No. 1148, December 29, 1931, 10 a.m., from
                    the Minister in China, <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_75"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 75</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
